</math></span><img src="https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/2b402242bb9203b673fd4a3cc631fc7f21089b6a" class="mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert" aria-hidden="true" style="vertical-align: -0.338ex; width:4.938ex; height:2.343ex;" alt="{\displaystyle F^{p}H}"/></span> as in the second definition.<sup about="#mwt26" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-1"}}'><a href="#cite_note-1" id="mwQw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwRA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwRQ">[</span>1<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwRg">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwRw">For applications in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Algebraic_geometry" title="Algebraic geometry" id="mwSA">algebraic geometry</a>, namely, classification of complex projective varieties by their <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Period_mapping" title="Period mapping" id="mwSQ">periods</a>, the set of all Hodge structures of weight <span class="mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline" typeof="mw:Extension/math" about="#mwt27" id="mwSg" data-mw='{"name":"math","attrs":{},"body":{"extsrc":"n"}}'><span class="mwe-math-mathml-inline mwe-math-mathml-a11y" style="display: none;"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="{\displaystyle n}">
  <semantics>
    <mrow class="MJX-TeXAtom-ORD">
      <mstyle displaystyle="true" scriptlevel="0">
        <mi>n</mi>
      </mstyle>
    </mrow>
    <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\displaystyle n}</annotation>
  </semantics>
</math></span><img src="https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/a601995d55609f2d9f5e233e36fbe9ea26011b3b" class="mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert" aria-hidden="true" style="vertical-align: -0.338ex; width:1.395ex; height:1.676ex;" alt="{\displaystyle n}"/></span> on <span class="mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline" typeof="mw:Extension/math" about="#mwt28" id="mwSw" data-mw='{"name":"math","attrs":{},"body":{"extsrc":"H_{\\Z}"}}'><span class="mwe-math-mathml-inline mwe-math-mathml-a11y" style="display: none;"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="{\displaystyle H_{\mathbb {Z} }}">
  <semantics>
    <mrow class="MJX-TeXAtom-ORD">
      <mstyle displaystyle="true" scriptlevel="0">
        <msub>
          <mi>H</mi>
          <mrow class="MJX-TeXAtom-ORD">
            <mrow class="MJX-TeXAtom-ORD">
              <mi mathvariant="double-struck">Z</mi>
            </mrow>
          </mrow>
        </msub>
      </mstyle>
    </mrow>
    <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\displaystyle H_{\mathbb {Z} }}</annotation>
  </semantics>
</math></span><img src="https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/234b78b24e88ae30d843d3d7fcf2e24ff7d6702a" class="mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert" aria-hidden="true" style="vertical-align: -0.671ex; width:3.26ex; height:2.509ex;" alt="{\displaystyle H_{\mathbb {Z} }}"/></span> is too big. Using the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann_bilinear_relations" title="Riemann bilinear relations" class="mw-redirect" id="mwTA">Riemann bilinear relations</a>, in this case called <i id="mwTQ">Hodge Riemann bilinear relations</i>, it can be substantially simplified. A <b id="mwTg">polarized Hodge structure of weight <i id="mwTw">n</i> </b> consists of a Hodge structure <span class="mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline" typeof="mw:Extension/math" about="#mwt29" id="mwUA" data-mw='{"name":"math","attrs":{},"body":{"extsrc":"(H_{\\Z}, H^{p,q})"}}'><span class="mwe-math-mathml-inline mwe-math-mathml-a11y" style="display: none;"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="{\displaystyle (H_{\mathbb {Z} },H^{p,q})}">
  <semantics>
    <mrow class="MJX-TeXAtom-ORD">
